NOTES AND QUESTIONS ON MCCORMICK V. KOPMANN
1. Let’s first consider the legal rule at issue here. Compare the quoted Illinois statute with Rule 8(d). Would the outcome in McCormick v. Kopmann have been any different had the case been decided under the Federal Rules? [Hint: the answer is no.]

2. As a policy matter, why are courts so receptive to alternate and even inconsistent claims and defenses? Why not demand from pleading parties: “Pick the theory you believe, and do not plead the rest?”

3. Does the fact that Rule 8 explicitly permits allegations to be made “alternatively or hypothetically” and “regardless of consistency” affect how you think about the plausibility pleading standard?

4. Now let’s look at how the McCormick case was litigated. Lucy McCormick, the plaintiff, could have sued Kopmann and the Huls in separate actions. In each of the separate actions, McCormick could have presented a single, coherent theory of liability to the jury. Instead, she filed a single action that required presenting two inconsistent theories to the jury. Wouldn’t it have been wiser to pursue separate actions? What did McCormick gain from filing a single action?

5. The court in McCormick notes that there is no indication that the plaintiff knew whether her allegations against Kopmann, her allegations against the
259
Huls, or neither were true. Note that in 1956 (and today), during an autopsy a coroner can determine a person’s blood alcohol content at the time of death. What might that tell us about when and how parties might use rules permitting pleading in the alternative?

